kosha signal-varga

  -- signal processing: the mathematics of signals in time and frequency.
  -- a signal is any quantity that varies with time (or space): audio, video,
  -- radar, seismic, biomedical, communication, control.
  -- signal processing asks: how do we analyse, filter, and transform signals?
  --
  -- the duality at the heart of signal processing:
  --   every signal has a time-domain representation (amplitude vs time)
  --   AND a frequency-domain representation (amplitude vs frequency).
  --   these are two ways of seeing the same thing.
  --   Fourier transform converts between them:
  --     X(f) = ∫ x(t) e^(-j2πft) dt
  --   this is the most important transform in engineering.
  --   it reveals which frequencies are present in a signal.
  --
  -- sampling (Nyquist-Shannon theorem):
  --   to represent a signal digitally, sample at fs > 2 × fmax.
  --   below Nyquist rate → aliasing: high frequencies appear as low frequencies.
  --   audio: 20Hz-20kHz → fs = 44100 Hz (CD), 48000 Hz (professional).
  --   aliasing = corruption. no recovery once aliased.
  --
  -- filtering: modifying the frequency content of a signal.
  --   low-pass filter (LPF):  passes low frequencies, blocks high. smoothing.
  --   high-pass filter (HPF): passes high frequencies, blocks low. differentiation.
  --   band-pass filter (BPF): passes a range. tuning to a specific frequency.
  --   band-stop / notch:      removes a specific frequency (e.g. 50Hz mains hum).
  --   FIR filter: finite impulse response. linear phase. always stable.
  --   IIR filter: infinite impulse response. efficient. can be unstable.
  --
  -- convolution: the fundamental operation of linear signal processing.
  --   y(t) = x(t) * h(t) = ∫ x(τ) h(t-τ) dτ
  --   h(t) is the impulse response — the system's "fingerprint".
  --   convolution in time = multiplication in frequency (Fourier convolution theorem).
  --   FIR filtering IS convolution with filter coefficients.
  --
  -- Z-transform: discrete-time equivalent of Laplace transform.
  --   H(z) = Σ h[n] z^(-n). poles and zeros determine filter behaviour.
  --   system stability: all poles inside unit circle.
  --
  -- DSP (digital signal processing):
  --   everything implemented in discrete time (samples, not continuous).
  --   FFT (Fast Fourier Transform): O(N log N) algorithm for DFT. Cooley-Tukey 1965.
  --   without FFT: O(N²) → too slow for real-time. with FFT: real-time audio possible.
  --
  -- applications:
  --   audio: equaliser, reverb, noise cancellation, compression, synthesis.
  --   communications: modulation, demodulation, channel equalisation, error correction.
  --   image: JPEG compression, edge detection, noise reduction.
  --   control: PID controller, kalman filter, model predictive control.
  --   biomedical: ECG filtering, EEG analysis, MRI reconstruction.
  --   radar/sonar: matched filtering, beamforming, CFAR detection.

  "engineering-varga-vishesa"
  "subanta-swarupa"

